<BaseLayout title="PC6 (Neiguan) — Inner Pass Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate PC6 (Neiguan) — the Inner Pass point. Benefits include nausea relief, anxiety reduction, and motion sickness support." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "PC6 (Neiguan)", href: "/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2025-02-15" dateModified="2026-05-15" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">PC6 (Neiguan) — Inner Pass</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">PC6, known as Neiguan or Inner Pass, is the premier acupressure point for nausea and anxiety. Located on the inner forearm, it is widely recommended for motion sickness, morning sickness, and chemotherapy-related nausea.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/pc6-location.webp" alt="PC6 acupressure point location on the inner forearm" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">PC6 is on the inner forearm, approximately 2 cun (about three finger-widths) below the wrist crease, between the two prominent tendons (palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis). It lies on the Pericardium meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Nausea and vomiting:</strong> Highly effective for motion sickness, morning sickness during pregnancy, and chemotherapy-induced nausea.</li>
      <li><strong>Anxiety and stress:</strong> PC6 calms the mind and may help reduce symptoms of panic and general anxiety.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-surgery recovery:</strong> It is commonly used in hospitals to reduce post-operative nausea.</li>
      <li><strong>Chest tightness:</strong> PC6 relieves chest pressure, palpitations, and breathlessness.</li>
      <li><strong>Digestive upset:</strong> It helps regulate stomach Qi and can ease indigestion and hiccups.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage PC6</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Hold your arm with the palm facing up.</li>
      <li>Place your opposite thumb on PC6, between the two tendons.</li>
      <li>Apply firm pressure with your thumb while gently rotating your wrist.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30?0 seconds, breathing slowly and deeply.</li>
      <li>Repeat on the other arm as needed.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2018 Cochrane review of 59 trials involving 6,000+ participants found that PC6 acupressure significantly reduced nausea and vomiting compared to sham treatment.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29384260/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Autonomic Neuroscience</em> showed that PC6 stimulation modulates gastric motility via the vagus nerve.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28964650/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2019 fMRI study in <em>Brain Research Bulletin</em> demonstrated that PC6 acupressure activates the hypothalamus and insula, areas associated with autonomic regulation and emotional processing.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31421177/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Apply gentle pressure ?avoid pressing too hard on the tendons or nerves.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have a wrist injury, carpal tunnel syndrome, or recent surgery in the area.</li>
      <li>Pregnant women (first trimester) may use PC6 safely for morning sickness, but consult your healthcare provider first.</li>
      <li>Stop if you experience numbness, tingling, or sharp pain.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">PC6 pairs well with <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point">HT7 (Shenmen)</a> for anxiety and with <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point">ST36 (Zusanli)</a> for digestive support. For motion sickness specifically, combine PC6 with <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point">LI4 (Hegu)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
